> Everyone works on Master/main branch.

> The most basic workflow possible.

> The simplest collaborative workflow is to have everyone  work on the master branch
  ( or main, or any other SINGLE branch)

> It's straight forward and can work for tiny teams, but it has quit a few
  shortcomings!

> Example, there are three collaborators and 1 performs three commits and other two
  working on another feature and tries to push the code on Github.
  "Failed to push. updates were rejected because the tip of your current branch is
   behind its remote counterpart. Merge the remote changes (e.g. "git pull")
   before pushing again."

**********************************************************************************

The Problem

> While it's nice and easy to only work on the master branch, this leads to some
  serious issues on teams!

> Lots of time spent resolving conflicts and merging code, especially as team size
  scales up.

> No one can work on anything without disturbing the main codebase. How do you add
  something radically different in? how do you experiment?

> The only way to collaborate on a feature together with another is push incomplete
  code to master. Other teammate now have broken code...
